  "query_preview": "..Shortwave ridge will keep us dry for one more day\nbefore the next system currently sliding down the California coast\nmakes its way into the area. Timing of the arrival of this system\nlooks to have slowed down a few hours so now expecting most\nactivity to start moving in early Tuesday morning versus Monday\nnight. With the later arrival and the departure time of this \nsystem remaining about the same at Tuesday night, QPF has trended\ndown a little. Amounts still look to be in the quarter to half \ninch range in the western half of our CWA, while eastern zones \ndon't fare quite as well with only about a tenth of an inch \nexpected. This is still a slightly cooler system with snow levels\nstarting at about 9000ft, but dropping down to about 6500-7000ft\nMonday night, however by the time that happens most of the\nmoisture will have moved east so only the Kaibab and the highest\nelevations of the San Francisco Peaks really stand to get anything\nover an inch. Wind will be a concern Tuesday afternoon with\nsouthwest winds 20-30 mph with gusts 40-45 mph. May be looking at\nissuing a Wind Advisory for areas northeast of the Mogollon Rim\nand covering most of northeast Arizona in upcoming shifts.\n\nBy Wednesday things dry out again, but temperatures won't recover\nmuch with afternoon highs remaining about 5 degrees below average.\nBy Thursday afternoon the next Pacific Storm moves into the area\nand this will be colder than the last storm, but at this time QPF\nlooks lower than the Tuesday storm system. The trend on QPF also\nlooks to be dropping at this time as the storm duration looks to\nbe shorter than previous runs, getting out of here now Friday\nafternoon or evening versus Saturday afternoon. Snow levels are \nexpected to drop to 6000ft with some minor accumulations possible \nabove 6-7000ft. Could be some slick conditions on the highest\npasses with this dusting Friday morning but at this time no major\ntravel disruptions are expected.\n\nSaturday looks to be a quiet day with highs remaining about 5\ndegrees below average. Sunday there's the potential for another\nsystem to move through with the GFS saying yes and the ECMWF\nsaying no.  Something to watch for the next few days.",
